  "content": {
    "id": "p7-q13-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "13",
    "prompt": "Suppose a line is perpendicular to a radius of a circle at its outer endpoint. Prove that the line is tangent to the circle. Hint: Assume the line is not tangent, and derive a contradiction.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "The line is tangent at the radius endpoint.\n\nAny other point $B$ on the perpendicular line satisfies $OB^2=OA^2+AB^2>OA^2$, so it lies outside the circle.",
      "worked": "Let $O$ be the centre and $A$ the outer endpoint of the radius. Suppose the line is not tangent. Since it contains $A$, it must enter the circle, so it contains another point $B$ on or inside the circle.\n\nAs $OA$ is perpendicular to $AB$, Pythagoras gives\n$$\\begin{align*}OB^2&=OA^2+AB^2\\\\OB^2&>OA^2\\\\OB&>OA.\\end{align*}$$\nThus $B$ is outside the circle, a contradiction. The line meets the circle only at $A$ and is therefore tangent.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 76
  },
